  "notes": [
    "Both VDD pins (11 and 32) and both VSS pins (12 and 31) must be connected. Wiring only one pair gives you a chip that half works and resets randomly.",
    "MCLR needs a 10k pull-up to VDD. There is no internal one by default, and a floating MCLR means a chip that never comes out of reset or resets on a sneeze.",
    "RA4 is open drain. It can sink but not source, so it cannot drive an LED to ground the way every other pin can. This is the single most asked question about this chip.",
    "Port A and Port E default to ANALOG at power-on. Until you write the ADCON1 register, digital reads on those pins return zero. The classic first-project bug.",
    "RB3 is the low-voltage programming pin. Tie it low if you are not using LVP, or noise can drop the chip into programming mode mid-run.",
    "RB4 to RB7 have interrupt-on-change, which is how keypads and wake-from-sleep are normally done.",
    "RB6 and RB7 are the ICSP clock and data. Anything hung on them must not fight the programmer, so keep loads light or add series resistors.",
    "Superseded for new designs by the PIC16F1939 and the PIC18F family, but still in production and still what a lot of university courses teach."
  ],
